 page('atlas/',{title:'Claim Transmission Atlas',description:'Explore the frozen time, taxonomy and state map. Read the two-clock study and inspect its source tables.',active:'atlas/',type:'research atlas explorer',eyebrow:'Explore the Atlas',toc:[{href:'#atlas-view',label:'The full map'},{href:'#reading',label:'How to read it'},{href:'#findings',label:'The bounded result'},{href:'#source-tables',label:'Source tables'},{href:'#paper',label:'Read the paper'}],body:`<header class="reader-header"><p class="eyebrow">Claim Transmission Atlas · v1.0</p><h1>One record.<br>Two clocks.</h1><p class="lede">How faithfully did the press transmit the record?<br>Follow the distinctions through time.</p><p class="edition-meta">Ben Amuwo · With Apart Research · September 2026</p><div class="actions">${link('atlas/paper/','Read the paper ↗','button primary')}${link('assets/figures/atlas.svg','Original SVG ↓','button')}</div></header><section id="atlas-view" class="atlas-section"><div class="section-bar"><h2>The Claim Transmission Atlas</h2><span>Time / taxonomy / state</span></div><p class="viewer-boundary">Lines connect claims, articles and evidence states. They do not imply article-to-article transmission or causal ancestry.</p><div class="atlas-viewer" data-viewer><div class="viewer-toolbar"><button data-zoom="in" aria-label="Zoom in">＋</button><button data-zoom="out" aria-label="Zoom out">−</button><button data-fit>Fit / reset</button><button data-pan aria-pressed="false">Pan mode</button><span data-scale role="status" aria-live="polite">100%</span><a href="${BASE}assets/figures/atlas.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Full-size ↗</a></div><div class="viewer-window" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Atlas figure viewer" aria-describedby="viewer-help"><img src="${BASE}assets/figures/atlas.svg" width="5000" height="3620" alt="Claim Transmission Atlas: four claim trunks, 87 article-claim trees, represented surface and body units, and Clock A and B states. Use the source tables below for a text alternative." draggable="false"></div><div class="viewer-pan-controls"><button data-move="left" aria-label="Pan left">←</button><button data-move="up" aria-label="Pan up">↑</button><button data-move="down" aria-label="Pan down">↓</button><button data-move="right" aria-label="Pan right">→</button></div></div><p class="reading-note" id="viewer-help">Zoom with + / −, then use the arrow controls or drag in Pan mode. With the viewer focused, use + / −, arrow keys and Home to reset. Pan mode enables touch dragging and pinch zoom; turn it off to scroll the page normally.</p><noscript><p>The full vector remains visible. <a href="${BASE}assets/figures/atlas.svg">Open the original SVG</a> to enlarge it.</p></noscript></section><section class="prose" id="reading"><p class="eyebrow">The visual grammar</p><h2>How to read the map</h2><p>A claim trunk leads to an article twig, then a SURFACE/BODY fork, a Clock-A node and a Clock-B node. Publication dates anchor articles in time. Local spacing within each article’s branch serves legibility; it does not encode another time interval.</p><div class="clock-pair"><div><span class="eyebrow">Clock A</span><h3>What was public then?</h3><p>Fidelity to the record available when the article appeared, under the frozen grace rule.</p></div><div><span class="eyebrow">Clock B</span><h3>What was known later?</h3><p>Fidelity to the later frozen record. Fair early reporting can become stale as evidence changes.</p></div></div><p>The trunk’s warm-to-cool colour tracks the visible public-record window. <strong>July 16 marks victim disclosure / the visible-time origin, not the incident date.</strong> Article colour identifies the frozen corpus stratum; shapes and fill carry evidence state.</p><ul class="stratum-key"><li><i class="general"></i> General</li><li><i class="business"></i> Business / mainstream technology</li><li><i class="specialist"></i> Specialist technology / cybersecurity</li></ul><p class="reading-note">Expanded 87-tree legibility export from the frozen submission assets. The complete legend is inside the figure. ${link('about/#atlas-selection','Selection and provenance →')}</p></section><section class="prose" id="findings"><p class="eyebrow">The bounded result</p><h2>Most distinctions survived.</h2><p>Established contemporaneous material drift occurred in <strong>7 of 83 assessable BODY observations (8.4%)</strong>. Both BODY BORDERLINE cases were adjudicated to non-drift; treating both as drift gives an upper sensitivity bound of 10.8%.</p>${table(['Claim family','Assessable N','Faithful','Drift','Borderline','Adjudicated','Upper bound'],family.slice(1).map(r=>[r[1],r[2],r[3],r[4],r[5],r[13]+'%',r[11]+'%']),'Clock A · BODY observations by claim family')}<p>Four of seven established BODY drifts concerned motive and objective. All 23 assessable BODY C5 observations were faithful at Clock A. Eight represented BODY observations were faithful at Clock A but drift at Clock B.</p><p class="qualification">Descriptive results from a bounded, outlet-weighted corpus of 24 English-language articles. Search completeness is unverified. The study does not support outlet rankings, journalist-intent claims or population-level inference.</p></section><section id="source-tables"><p class="eyebrow">Accessible record</p><h2>Inspect the source tables</h2><p>Seven frozen submission tables, reproduced directly. Column names and denominators are preserved; CSV downloads carry the original bytes.</p><div class="source-tables">${sourceTables}</div></section><section id="paper" class="reader-download"><p class="eyebrow">The complete study</p><h2>How Faithfully Did the Press Transmit the Record?</h2><p>A Preregistered Two-Clock Audit of Claim Drift in Coverage of the July 2026 OpenAI-Hugging Face Incident</p>${downloads('atlas')}${link('atlas/paper/','Open the full-paper reader →','text-link')}</section>`});
