 page('about/',{title:'About the editions',description:'Publication sources, reading editions and the limits of this presentation.',type:'research',toc:[{href:'#editions',label:'Reading editions'},{href:'#atlas-selection',label:'Atlas selection'},{href:'#illustrations',label:'Illustrations'},{href:'#source-boundary',label:'Source boundaries'}],body:`<header class="reader-header"><p class="eyebrow">A note on the record</p><h1>Sources remain<br>sources.</h1><p class="lede">This website presents two publications and a narrative. It does not recompute their research or revise their findings.</p></header><article class="prose"><section id="editions"><h2>Reading editions</h2><p>The Minded-Language Audit uses the author-supplied nine-page reading edition: dark-on-dark text was repaired and the final review-items page was removed before this website was built. The retained research content and figures are preserved.</p><p>The Atlas reader is derived from the supplied six-page final PDF. The repository’s differently worded report Markdown was not used. The submission-draft running footers and the Author Review Items section have since been resolved by the author; the canonical website and repository links are provided separately.</p><p>Both downloadable PDFs add clickable project and repository URLs in the top margin on every page. Their body text, figures and pagination are retained. The Markdown editions reflow PDF text into paragraphs and tables and link to the figures. The companion ZIP keeps the Markdown and figure paths together for offline use.</p></section><section id="atlas-selection"><h2>Atlas selection</h2><p>The explorer uses the unmodified <code>submission/assets/claim_transmission_atlas.svg</code> at research revision <code>2e97880</code>. This is the expanded 5000 × 3620 legibility export with 87 article-claim trees. The older temperature-axis-named export contains 85 trees and was not selected.</p><p>The chronology correction and later legibility notes establish the interpretation: July 16 is victim disclosure and the visible-time origin; it is not the incident date. The later notes document semantic comparison against current coding, while explicitly qualifying the Artist’s access to the earlier parent bytes. This website’s inventory checks do not independently re-certify the study’s approval process.</p><p class="hash">SVG SHA-256<br>eac1e82817728b4df34d5455c20fdc2390a5d51b391693f10a9af72dc01a6f67</p></section><section id="illustrations"><h2>The illustrations</h2><p>All 23 images are paired by their supplied Roman-numeral or prologue/epilogue stem. Compressed WebP copies retain the original dimensions. The supplied files are 357–486 pixels wide; larger displays will reveal their softness. Full-resolution replacements can be added without changing the story or chapter links.</p><p>The complete source narrative is preserved, including its distinct voice, poetry and emphasis. The supplied preparatory text had already removed chat citation markers. The story’s original title is <em>With Effort, to the Stars</em>; “The Translator’s Tale” names this part of the website.</p></section><section id="source-boundary"><h2>Source boundaries</h2><p>The audit’s printed total of 178,909 words includes the human- and model-authored segmented subsets alongside the full internal record. The site preserves that source count without relabelling it as a deduplicated corpus size. The paper’s comparisons and confidence language remain the authors’ claims.</p><p>The source papers refer to larger research and audit packages. This publication site includes the selected papers, dossier, figures, seven frozen submission tables and story. It does not mirror worker archives or claim to contain the entire audit computation package.</p><p>${link('assets/source-manifest.json','Download the publication asset manifest →')}</p></section></article>`});
